{"records":[{"id":"jkj7x7pjkg","title":"Low Prevalence of High-Risk Human Papillomavirus in Nail Unit Squamous Cell Carcinoma in Koreans using Next-generation Sequencing","description":"Supplementary materials of the article: \"Low Prevalence of High-Risk Human Papillomavirus in Nail Unit Squamous Cell Carcinoma in Koreans using Next-generation Sequencing\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-15T12:55:48.371Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jkj7x7pjkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jkj7x7pjkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Je-Ho","last_name":"Mun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Virus","Skin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"88fcgj65bf","title":"Association of Human Papillomavirus and Extra-genital Bowen’s Disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in situ): A Systematic Review","description":"Supplementary materials for publication JAAD-D-20-02223 (Namgoong et al. Association of Human Papillomavirus and Extra-genital Bowen’s Disease (Squamous Cell Carcinoma in situ): A Systematic Review)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-20T13:19:57.542Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/88fcgj65bf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/88fcgj65bf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sik","last_name":"Namgoong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jaeyoung","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kyung Muk","last_name":"Jeong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiehyun","last_name":"Jeon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hae Jun","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoo Sang","last_name":"Baek","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"55yjccgfg9","title":"Optimal posttreatment surveillance strategy for human papillomavirus-positive oropharyngeal cancer. Caineng Cao et al.","description":"human papillomavirus-positive oropharyngeal cancer","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-19T10:02:26.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/55yjccgfg9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/55yjccgfg9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Caineng","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nancy Y.","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"National Institutes of Health/National Cancer Institute Cancer Center Support Grant ","grant_id":"P30 CA008748"}],"categories":["Oropharyngeal Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9ds7g93p7n","title":"Codon Usage Bias Analysis of Human Papillomavirus 18’s L1 Protein and its Host Adaptability","description":"Supplementary table 1: Nucleotides composition of L1 protein of Human Papillomavirus 18 genome at third codon position (A3s, T3s, G3s, and C3s) and GC1, GC2, and GC3 (Nucleotide present GC present at the first, second, and third position of codon) and GC3s (Nucleotide present at the third position of codon). CAI (Codon Adaptation Index), CBI (Codon Bias Index), FOP (Frequency of optimal codons), Nc (Effective Number), GRAVY and AROMO values of L1 protein of Human Papillomavirus 18 genome. G3/(A3+T3), A3/(A3+T3), and RCDI values of L1 protein of Human Papillomavirus 18 genome. \nSupplementary table 2: Values of Relative Synonymous Codon Usage (RSCU) for accession number from KU707718.1 to KU707825.1 of L1 protein of Human Papillomavirus 18. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-13T07:01:22.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9ds7g93p7n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9ds7g93p7n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"vinaya","last_name":"vindo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Adaptation","Human Papillomavirus","Codon Usage"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bs3dnpr2g4","title":"Gasdermin D maintains bone mass by rewiring the endo-lysosomal pathway of osteoclastic bone resorption ","description":"Gasdermin D (GSDMD)-mediated pyroptosis induces immunogenic cell death and promotes inflammation. However, the functions of GSDMD in tissue homeostasis remain unclear. Here, we identify a physiological function of GSDMD in osteoclasts via a non-lytic p20 generated protein, which prevents bone loss to maintain bone homeostasis. In the late stage of RANKL-induced osteoclastogenesis, GSDMD underwent cleavage dependent on RIPK1 and caspase-8/-3 to yield this p20 product. Gsdmd-deficient osteoclasts showed normal differentiation but enhanced bone resorption with excessive lysosomal activity. Mice with complete or myeloid-specific Gsdmd deletion exhibited increased trabecular bone loss and more severe aging/ovariectomy-induced osteoporosis. GSDMD p20 was preferentially localized to early endosomes and limited endo-lysosomal trafficking and maturation, relying on its oligomerization and control of phosphoinositide conversion by binding to phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate (PI(3)P). We have thus identified an anti-osteoclastic function of GSDMD as a checkpoint for lysosomal maturation and secretion, and linked this to bone homeostasis and endosome-lysosome biology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-23T20:42:12.308Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bs3dnpr2g4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bs3dnpr2g4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mobai","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dehang","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Lysosomes","Osteoclast","Endosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m4hzpgbzdx","title":"Utility of Human Papillomavirus Vaccination for Keratinocyte Carcinomas: A Literature Review","description":"We reviewed the literature to evaluate the role of human papillomavirus vaccination for keratinocyte carcinoma and actinic keratoses. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-01T11:07:57.125Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m4hzpgbzdx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m4hzpgbzdx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jennifer","last_name":"Su","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology","Skin Cancer","Human Papillomavirus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"txnvtdw2zs","title":"GPI-Anchored Ligand-BioID2-Tagging System Identifies Galectin-1 Mediating Zika Virus Entry Gao et al","description":"Identification of host factors facilitating pathogen entry is critical for preventing infectious diseases. Here, we report a tagging system consisting of a viral receptor-binding protein (RBP) linked to BioID2, which is expressed on the cell surface via a GPI anchor. Using VSV or Zika virus (ZIKV) RBP, the system (BioID2- RBP(V)-GPI; BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI) faithfully identifies LDLR and AXL, the receptors of VSV and ZIKV, respectively. Being GPI-anchored is essential for the probe to function properly. Furthermore, BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI expressed in human neuronal progenitor cells identifies galectin-1 on cell surface pivotal for ZIKV entry. This conclusion is further supported by antibody blocking and galectin-1 silencing in A549 and mouse neural cells. Importantly, Lgas1-/- mice are significantly more resistant to ZIKV infection than Lgas1+/+ littermates are, having significantly lower virus titers and fewer pathologies in various organs. This tagging system may have broad applications for identifying protein-protein interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T19:31:31.48Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/txnvtdw2zs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/txnvtdw2zs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shanshan","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4pyjj93dt","title":"A Study of A lysosomal DRAM1–GLUT1–NADPH axis coordinates glucose sensing with metabolic reprogramming. Jia et al","description":"Perturbed glucose homeostasis is a hallmark of metabolic disease and cancer, and cellular responses to excess glucose are tightly controlled. We report here a lysosomal glucose sensing axis linking the transporter GLUT1 with the lysosomal protein DRAM1. Pathophysiological glucose induces DRAM1 via the MLX/ChREBP transcriptional complex. DRAM1 then binds newly synthesized GLUT1 and promotes its traffic to lysosomes without degradation, reducing surface GLUT1 and acting as a reversible brake on glucose entry. Hexokinase or glucose-6-phosphate (G6P) dehydrogenase disruption weakens DRAM1-GLUT1 complex formation, whereas NADPH oxidase (NOX) inhibition enhances assembly, implicating nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH) as a key metabolic signal downstream of elevated glucose. Sequestration of GLUT1 suppresses glucose uptake and glycolysis and rewires metabolism toward glutamine anaplerosis, creating vulnerability to glutaminase (GLS) inhibition. In vivo, Dram1−/− tumors display a glycolytic metabolite profile, consistent with loss of this brake. Together, these data define an mTORC1- and AMPK-independent lysosomal mechanism that protects metabolic homeostasis under hyperglycemic conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-06T16:30:23.73Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4pyjj93dt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4pyjj93dt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jia","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"28s3bkk36g","title":"FDG_multiomics_in_HNSCC","description":"In this study we combined whole exome sequencing and clinical 18F-FDG imaging data to identify novel therapeutic options for human papillomavirus (HPV)-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma in a minimally-invasive manner. The data provided here can aid further identification of novel association between genetic and imaging data. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-04T14:48:03.233Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/28s3bkk36g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/28s3bkk36g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Stoiber","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Radiomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6d8w9vv5bv","title":"Lysosomal LAMP proteins regulate lysosomal pH by direct inhibition of PD-related TMEM175 channel-Jiyuan Zhang","description":"These are the raw image data related to the manuscript submitted to Molecular Cell titled \"Lysosomal LAMP proteins regulate lysosomal pH by direct inhibition of PD-related TMEM175 channel\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-22T08:22:05.933Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6d8w9vv5bv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6d8w9vv5bv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Youxing","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiyuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Image Database"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129773,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":102,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129773,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":129255,"restricted":64,"embargoed":454},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2171},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1333},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":619},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":459},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":431},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":410},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":298},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":265}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":127164},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1898},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":184},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":134},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":62},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":872},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":769},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusan